x86_64-linux  a closure type has no name the standard obliges anybody to match - [expr.prim.lambda] calls it a unique unnamed type - and the two compilers spell theirs differently, `$_N` against clang's `UlvE_`; lambda.nonames records it. Nothing links against a closure: its call operator is reached through the object, which never leaves the function that made it. Every closure number agrees with clang here, the one met while `n`'s body was read for its return type being named apart and never emitted; only the spelling of the nested one differs.
arm64-darwin  the same, with Mach-O's leading underscore.
x86_64-windows  the same difference in closure names: clang for this ABI spells a closure `<lambda_N>` in a `?0?` scope and writes its deduced return as `@`, where cpp11 writes `$_N` in `?1?` and the deduced type. Nothing links against a closure type.
